WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.929 --> 00:00:18.159
The to the secret garden had to be postponed for a week.

2
00:00:19.501 --> 00:00:21.062
First the weather was windy.

3
00:00:22.323 --> 00:00:24.825
Then Colin seemed to be coming down with a cold.

4
00:00:26.066 --> 00:00:29.929
In the old days, these two events would have thrown him into a rage.

5
00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:35.353
But Mary visited him every day, and Dickon also came in a few times.

6
00:00:36.713 --> 00:00:40.974
Dickon described what was happening on the moor and in the hedges and on the riverbanks.

7
00:00:42.434 --> 00:00:48.816
Colin almost trembled with excitement when Dickon told him about otter houses and bird nests and rabbit burrows.

8
00:00:48.836 --> 00:00:55.798
"The animals are really just like us," Dickon said, "except they have to build their houses every year."

9
00:00:57.258 --> 00:01:02.860
As each day passed, Colin seemed to grow more excited about the mystery surrounding the secret garden.

10
00:01:04.192 --> 00:01:07.873
The children did not want anyone to see them when they finally took him there.

11
00:01:09.174 --> 00:01:12.154
They had long talks, planning which paths they would take.

12
00:01:13.515 --> 00:01:17.156
They wanted everyone to think that they were just looking at the new flowers.

13
00:01:20.297 --> 00:01:22.898
One day Colin summoned the head gardener to his room.

14
00:01:24.178 --> 00:01:26.419
Mr. Roach was startled when he opened the door.

15
00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:32.040
A crow announced his arrival with a loud caw!

16
00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.702
Colin was sitting up in a chair, watching Dickon feed a lamb with a bottle.

17
00:01:35.722 --> 00:01:41.366
"I sent for you," said Colin, "to give you some very important orders."

18
00:01:41.386 --> 00:01:43.907
"Very good, sir."

19
00:01:44.307 --> 00:01:47.409
Mr. Roach smiled to himself at Colin's royal manners.

20
00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.412
"I am going out in my wheelchair this afternoon," Colin went on.

21
00:01:52.432 --> 00:01:56.955
"If the fresh air agrees with me, I may go out every day.

22
00:01:58.255 --> 00:02:02.815
When I do, none of the gardeners is to be anywhere near the long path by the walled

23
00:02:02.855 --> 00:02:10.042
gardens. I will go out about two o'clock, and everyone must keep away until I send word that

24
00:02:10.062 --> 00:02:11.423
they may go back to work."

25
00:02:11.443 --> 00:02:14.306
"Very good, sir," Mr.

26
00:02:14.346 --> 00:02:15.106
Roach said again.

27
00:02:16.568 --> 00:02:17.769
Colin turned to Mary.

28
00:02:17.789 --> 00:02:21.832
"What is that thing you say in India when you have finished talking?"

29
00:02:23.073 --> 00:02:25.696
"You say, 'You have my permission to go.'"

30
00:02:27.624 --> 00:02:29.745
Colin, the rajah, waved his hand.

31
00:02:29.765 --> 00:02:34.126
"You have my permission to go," he said to Mr.

32
00:02:34.186 --> 00:02:40.028
Roach. That afternoon the strongest footman carried Colin downstairs.

33
00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:46.070
The servant put Colin in his wheelchair and fixed his cushions and blankets around him.

34
00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.031
Dickon began to push the wheelchair slowly and steadily.

35
00:02:52.451 --> 00:02:53.612
Mary walked beside it.

36
00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:57.821
Colin leaned back and lifted his face to the sky.

37
00:02:57.841 --> 00:03:02.602
"There are so many sounds of singing and humming," he said.

38
00:03:02.622 --> 00:03:07.644
"What is that sweet scent I smell on every puff of air?"

39
00:03:07.664 --> 00:03:12.005
"It's the flowers on the moor," said Dickon.

40
00:03:13.905 --> 00:03:17.926
It turned out that the children didn't have to worry about seeing anyone.

41
00:03:19.407 --> 00:03:22.928
Not a single human creature was to be found anywhere near the gardens.

42
00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.382
But they followed their carefully planned route anyway, just for the fun of it.

43
00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:33.424
They wound in and out among the shrubbery and around the flower beds.

44
00:03:35.465 --> 00:03:40.567
When they finally reached the long path by the walled gardens, they began to speak in whispers.

45
00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:47.030
"This is where I used to walk up and down and wonder about the secret garden," said Mary.

46
00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:48.770
"Is it?"

47
00:03:49.211 --> 00:03:51.952
Colin began to search the ivy for signs of the door.

48
00:03:53.964 --> 00:03:55.025
"But I don't see anything.

49
00:03:56.126 --> 00:03:56.946
There is no door."

50
00:03:56.966 --> 00:04:00.949
"That's what I thought," said Mary.

51
00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:03.951
They walked on in silence.

52
00:04:05.392 --> 00:04:09.114
"This is where the robin flew over the wall," Mary whispered.

53
00:04:09.134 --> 00:04:11.816
"Oh, I wish he'd come again!"

54
00:04:11.836 --> 00:04:19.041
"And that," said Mary, pointing under a bush, "is where he perched on the heap of dirt and

55
00:04:19.081 --> 00:04:19.962
showed me the key."

56
00:04:21.663 --> 00:04:23.024
Colin suddenly sat up.

57
00:04:24.224 --> 00:04:25.405
"Where? There?"

58
00:04:25.885 --> 00:04:26.485
He pointed.

59
00:04:27.886 --> 00:04:29.247
Dickon stopped the wheelchair.

60
00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:35.750
"And this," said Mary, "is where I went to talk to him, and where the wind blew aside

61
00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:36.631
the curtain of ivy.

62
00:04:37.671 --> 00:04:38.572
And here is the door.

63
00:04:39.292 --> 00:04:40.893
Dickon, push him in quickly!"

64
00:04:42.514 --> 00:04:44.915
Dickon did just that with one strong push.

65
00:04:46.276 --> 00:04:48.097
Colin leaned back against his cushions.

66
00:04:49.458 --> 00:04:53.640
He covered his eyes with his hands until they were inside and the door shut behind them.

67
00:04:54.928 --> 00:04:56.329
Then he dropped his hands.

68
00:04:57.709 --> 00:05:02.131
He looked around the garden just as Mary and Dickon had done the first time they were inside.

69
00:05:03.872 --> 00:05:07.473
A green veil of little leaves had crept over the walls and trees.

70
00:05:08.934 --> 00:05:12.495
Everywhere on the ground there were touches of gold and purple and white.

71
00:05:13.856 --> 00:05:17.678
The trees were showing their pink and white blossoms above Colin's head.

72
00:05:19.038 --> 00:05:23.140
He could hear the fluttering of wings and the singing of birds and the humming of bees.

73
00:05:24.576 --> 00:05:26.318
The sun felt warm on his face.

74
00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.442
Mary and Dickon stood and stared at Colin.

75
00:05:31.743 --> 00:05:36.588
He looked so different because a pink glow of color had crept all over his pale face and

76
00:05:36.648 --> 00:05:39.491
hands. "I will get well!"

77
00:05:40.011 --> 00:05:40.772
Colin cried.

78
00:05:40.792 --> 00:05:43.114
"Mary! Dickon!

79
00:05:43.595 --> 00:05:44.616
I will get well!

80
00:05:45.817 --> 00:05:48.360
And I will live forever and ever and ever!"
